10:32:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: They’re two different naming traditions for the same musical pitches: - **Letters (C–D–E–F–G–A–B)** are the older Western pitch-name system. They label specific notes on the musical staff: for example, C is always C (though its exact frequency can vary slightly with tuning). - **Do–re–mi–fa–sol–la–si** is **solfège**, a system of sung syllables developed mainly to teach sight-singing, melody, and relationships between notes. In many countries, especially Romance-language ones, solfège is used as the ordinary note-name system: | Letter system | Solfège name | |---|---| | C | do | | D | re | | E | mi | | F | fa | | G | sol | | A | la | | B | si | So in that “fixed-do” usage, **do always means C**, re always means D, and so on. But in some teaching traditions—especially English-speaking ones—solfège is often **movable-do**: **do means the tonic**, or home note, of the current key. In C major, C is do; in D major, D is do. This helps singers hear musical functions and intervals rather than memorize absolute pitches. The syllables have a medieval origin. Guido of Arezzo used the opening syllables of lines from a Latin hymn: *ut–re–mi–fa–sol–la*. Later, **ut** became **do** because it is easier to sing, and **si** was added for the seventh scale degree. In some English-language teaching, **si** is called **ti** to make every syllable begin with a different letter. One small regional complication: in German-speaking music, **B** means B-flat, while B natural is called **H**.
